TopicRouting.UserPromptTemplate:
You are a dialogue retrieval tool. Analyze the latest NPC/player exchange and recent scene interaction history, then decide which topics are relevant.
{topic_list}

Routing hints:
{routing_guidance}

Scene interaction history (up to the previous 3 dialogue turns):
{history}

*Latest NPC/player exchange*:
NPC: {latest_npc}
Player: {latest_player}
Select exactly {top_n} closest topic codes in rule_codes. Also extract explicit third-party nouns from the latest exchange into mentioned_entities.entities. Put all named people/titles, places, families, factions, item/goods/equipment names or types, policy/law names, troop/unit/prisoner names or types, and other useful raw noun phrases in this single array; do not classify by type. When policies or laws are the subject, keep the complete policy/law noun phrase even when it is generic (for example, policy/law), and separately include every explicitly named country or faction so downstream retrieval preserves its scope. Do not extract current speakers or player names just because they are speakers. Order entities by retrieval importance: nouns central to the latest request/reply and explicit action targets first, incidental mentions later; for equal importance, put the newer mention first. Output one strict JSON object only: {"rule_codes":["CODE"],"mentioned_entities":{mentioned_entities_schema}}.

MemorySelection.UserPromptTemplate:
{mode_instruction}
Output JSON schema: {"rule_codes":[],"memory_ids":[1,2]}
Select exactly {final_count} memory_ids from the candidate list. If uncertain, choose the closest by semantic relevance. Do not select more than {final_count}.

Latest player input:
{latest_player_input}
Latest NPC/context input:
{latest_npc_input}
Current scene:
{current_scene}

Memory candidates, numbered by date from far to near:
{memory_candidates}

PlayerRpTemplateSelection.UserPromptTemplate:
Select one game template for a player-crafted RP item.
Input name: {requested_name}
Invested denars: {invested_denars}
Mode: {craft_mode}
Candidate count: {candidate_count}
Rows: rank|template_id|name|type|standard_price
{template_candidates}
Choose exactly one listed row. Prioritize name/type meaning; use price closeness only when meanings are similarly suitable. Never invent a row or ID. Output strict JSON only: {"candidate":1}
